WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.929
Welcome to part two: the fundamentals
of telecommunication, following

2
00:00:04.969 --> 00:00:09.878
Professor Barlabé's notes Introducció
a la Telecomunicació, part one.

3
00:00:09.928 --> 00:00:12.871
This is where most of the
short theory questions of

4
00:00:12.911 --> 00:00:15.795
the partial come from, so
we will go definition by

5
00:00:15.835 --> 00:00:19.752
definition, and solve every
related exam question on the way.

6
00:00:19.802 --> 00:00:23.726
The plan is: definitions and
the general model, types of

7
00:00:23.766 --> 00:00:27.478
signals and their characteristic
values, services and

8
00:00:27.518 --> 00:00:31.725
networks, then Fourier analysis,
linear systems and filters,

9
00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:37.636
modulation, distortion, noise, decibels,
and finally the Shannon capacity law.

10
00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:43.189
Etymologically telecommunication means
communication at a distance, but

11
00:00:43.229 --> 00:00:47.798
the course uses the definition of the
International Telecommunication Union.

12
00:00:47.848 --> 00:00:52.243
According to the I T U, telecommunication
is any transmission and

13
00:00:52.283 --> 00:00:56.882
reception of signs, signals, writing,
images, sounds and information

14
00:00:56.922 --> 00:01:01.979
of any kind by wire, radio, optical
or other electromagnetic systems.

15
00:01:02.029 --> 00:01:05.870
The key word is electromagnetic:
an African talking drum or smoke

16
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.797
signals are communication at a distance,
but they are not telecommunication.

17
00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:14.209
Information is anything that
increases our level of knowledge,

18
00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:18.221
and the message is the physical
manifestation of that information.

19
00:01:18.271 --> 00:01:20.917
A signal is a physical
magnitude whose value

20
00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:23.604
carries information; if
it is a voltage or a

21
00:01:23.644 --> 00:01:26.474
current we call it an
electrical signal, and we

22
00:01:26.514 --> 00:01:30.161
model it mathematically as
a function of time, x of t.

23
00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:34.508
A system is a set of components with
a purpose; for us it is a box that

24
00:01:34.548 --> 00:01:39.663
receives an input signal x of t and
delivers a different output signal y of t.

25
00:01:41.313 --> 00:01:44.985
Every telecommunication system
fits this block diagram: a

26
00:01:45.025 --> 00:01:49.477
source, a transmitter, a channel,
a receiver and a destination.

27
00:01:49.527 --> 00:01:53.000
The channel is the physical
medium: a transmission line

28
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.513
such as a two-wire cable, a
coaxial cable or an optical

29
00:01:56.553 --> 00:02:00.027
fibre, or the air and the
vacuum for radio, and that is

30
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:04.505
the only big classification of
telecommunication, line or radio.

31
00:02:04.555 --> 00:02:07.394
The two main characteristics
of a channel are its

32
00:02:07.434 --> 00:02:10.097
attenuation and its
bandwidth: every signal is

33
00:02:10.137 --> 00:02:14.600
attenuated as it propagates, and the
attenuation depends on frequency.

34
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.585
The transmitter conditions the signal
for the channel: it modulates,

35
00:02:18.625 --> 00:02:23.243
codes and encrypts it, and then amplifies
it to overcome the attenuation.

36
00:02:23.293 --> 00:02:27.471
The receiver gets a very weak signal,
so it first amplifies it and then

37
00:02:27.511 --> 00:02:32.257
undoes the transmitter's processing:
it demodulates, decodes and decrypts.

38
00:02:33.907 --> 00:02:36.893
Signals are first deterministic or random.

39
00:02:36.943 --> 00:02:41.394
A deterministic signal is fully specified
at every instant, like a cosine,

40
00:02:41.434 --> 00:02:46.639
while a random signal can take one of
several values with a certain probability.

41
00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:50.394
Strictly speaking, any signal
that carries information must

42
00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:54.139
be random, because if I already
know what you will say, you

43
00:02:54.179 --> 00:02:58.011
are not giving me information;
but random signals are hard to

44
00:02:58.051 --> 00:03:02.526
handle, so we model them with
deterministic ones whenever we can.

45
00:03:02.576 --> 00:03:05.961
An analog signal is continuous
in time and in value; a

46
00:03:06.001 --> 00:03:09.702
discrete signal has any value
but only at precise instants;

47
00:03:09.742 --> 00:03:14.594
and a digital signal has only a finite
set of values at those instants.

48
00:03:14.644 --> 00:03:19.104
A periodic signal repeats
identically every period T zero.

49
00:03:19.154 --> 00:03:23.118
Converting analog to digital and
back leaves a small difference, a

50
00:03:23.158 --> 00:03:27.121
distortion, which can be made
negligible; that is why today almost

51
00:03:27.161 --> 00:03:31.939
everything is transmitted digitally,
for a reason we will see with noise.

52
00:03:33.589 --> 00:03:35.919
Next, the characteristic values of a

53
00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:39.337
signal, which come up in
almost every partial.

54
00:03:39.387 --> 00:03:42.624
The peak value is the maximum
absolute value, and the

55
00:03:42.664 --> 00:03:45.839
mean value is the time
average, which for a periodic

56
00:03:45.879 --> 00:03:50.001
signal is the integral over one
period divided by the period.

57
00:03:50.051 --> 00:03:53.279
The instantaneous power is x
squared, which is the power

58
00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:56.782
dissipated in a one-ohm resistor;
with another resistance it

59
00:03:56.822 --> 00:04:00.908
is v squared over R for a voltage,
or i squared R for a current.

60
00:04:00.958 --> 00:04:06.003
The mean power is the average of the
instantaneous power, the rms value is

61
00:04:06.043 --> 00:04:11.021
its square root, and the energy is the
integral of x squared over all time;

62
00:04:11.061 --> 00:04:16.830
energy signals have finite energy, while
periodic signals have finite mean power.

63
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.498
Two exam classics follow directly:
in problem 3.1 the mean of A

64
00:04:21.538 --> 00:04:25.411
cosine plus B sine at the same
frequency is zero, because every

65
00:04:25.451 --> 00:04:30.007
sinusoid averages to zero over a
period, and in the 2016 partial

66
00:04:30.047 --> 00:04:35.179
the mean of B over 2 plus A cosine is
simply B over 2, the constant term.

67
00:04:36.829 --> 00:04:41.092
Telecommunication services are
classified by how they use the channel.

68
00:04:41.142 --> 00:04:44.601
Simplex means one direction only,
like television, or the link

69
00:04:44.641 --> 00:04:48.829
that carries processed radar
information to the controller's screen.

70
00:04:48.879 --> 00:04:52.256
Half-duplex is bidirectional
but not simultaneous, on the

71
00:04:52.296 --> 00:04:55.734
same channel, and it needs a
protocol to change direction:

72
00:04:55.774 --> 00:04:58.911
this is the pilot talking
with the controller, on one

73
00:04:58.951 --> 00:05:03.498
frequency, never both at once, ending
each message with the call sign.

74
00:05:03.548 --> 00:05:07.038
Full-duplex is bidirectional
and simultaneous, and it needs

75
00:05:07.078 --> 00:05:10.899
two channels, one for each
direction, as in the telephone.

76
00:05:10.949 --> 00:05:14.436
The question 'what kind of
communication is established between

77
00:05:14.476 --> 00:05:18.579
pilot and controller' appeared in
the 2016 partial and is on the

78
00:05:18.619 --> 00:05:22.665
question sheet: the answer is
half-duplex, and you should say why.

79
00:05:24.315 --> 00:05:29.030
Services are also classified by where
the transmitters and receivers are.

80
00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:32.069
Point to point means a single
transmitter and a single

81
00:05:32.109 --> 00:05:36.048
receiver in different places, like
a radio link or a phone call.

82
00:05:36.098 --> 00:05:40.036
Broadcast, radiodifusió, means
a few transmitters and many

83
00:05:40.076 --> 00:05:44.610
receivers, like television, or
a satellite navigation system.

84
00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:48.713
In a radar, the transmitter and the
receiver are in the same place.

85
00:05:48.763 --> 00:05:53.022
Another repeated exam question asks
whether an NDB with its ADF is

86
00:05:53.062 --> 00:05:56.952
point to point, broadcast or radar:
it is broadcast, because one

87
00:05:56.992 --> 00:06:01.640
non-directional beacon radiates and
any number of aircraft receive it.

88
00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:07.774
You cannot wire every telephone to every
other telephone, and not everybody talks

89
00:06:07.814 --> 00:06:12.743
at once, so terminals are interconnected
through networks with a few basic shapes.

90
00:06:12.793 --> 00:06:15.638
In a bus, all terminals
hang in parallel from

91
00:06:15.678 --> 00:06:18.913
one single transmission
line, as in Ethernet.

92
00:06:18.963 --> 00:06:23.806
In a ring, the line closes on
itself, as in IBM's token ring.

93
00:06:23.856 --> 00:06:27.407
In a star, every terminal
connects to one central point, a

94
00:06:27.447 --> 00:06:31.636
hub in a local network or an
exchange in the telephone system.

95
00:06:31.686 --> 00:06:34.783
A tree is the intermediate
case, a bus of buses, and

96
00:06:34.823 --> 00:06:38.824
the figure of question 7
of the 2022 partial, with

97
00:06:38.864 --> 00:06:42.532
small buses hanging from a
main bus, is exactly a tree.

98
00:06:42.582 --> 00:06:48.350
The 2023 partial asked you to draw
the three basic topologies, and the

99
00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:53.134
notes list bus, ring, star, tree and
hybrid, so to be safe sketch bus,

100
00:06:53.174 --> 00:06:58.512
ring, star and tree, and remember that
large networks use multiple stars.

101
00:07:00.162 --> 00:07:03.501
Telecommunication problems
are usually easier in the

102
00:07:03.541 --> 00:07:06.880
frequency domain than in
the time domain, and it all

103
00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:11.298
starts with the sinusoid A cosine
of two pi f zero t plus phi.

104
00:07:11.348 --> 00:07:15.682
A is the amplitude, f zero the
frequency, the inverse of the period,

105
00:07:15.722 --> 00:07:20.382
and phi the phase, which shifts the
peak away from the time origin.

106
00:07:20.432 --> 00:07:25.104
Using Euler's formula, e to the j
theta equals cosine theta plus j sine

107
00:07:25.144 --> 00:07:29.750
theta, the sinusoid is the real part
of a phasor: a vector of length A

108
00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:35.515
rotating counter-clockwise in the complex
plane at f zero revolutions per second.

109
00:07:35.565 --> 00:07:38.243
Watch the projection
of the rotating phasor

110
00:07:38.283 --> 00:07:41.604
on the real axis: it
traces exactly the cosine.

111
00:07:41.654 --> 00:07:44.579
So the whole sinusoid is
described by three numbers,

112
00:07:44.619 --> 00:07:47.487
and we can draw it in an
amplitude versus frequency

113
00:07:47.527 --> 00:07:50.955
diagram: a single spectral
line of height A at f zero.

114
00:07:51.005 --> 00:07:53.059
That diagram is the spectrum of the

115
00:07:53.099 --> 00:07:56.081
signal, its picture in
the frequency domain.

116
00:07:57.731 --> 00:08:02.066
Here is the example from the
notes: w of t equals 7 minus 10

117
00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:08.379
cosine of 40 pi t minus 60
degrees, plus 4 sine of 120 pi t.

118
00:08:08.429 --> 00:08:12.691
To read its spectrum, write
every term as a positive cosine.

119
00:08:12.741 --> 00:08:18.246
The 7 is a cosine at zero frequency,
the DC term, with amplitude 7.

120
00:08:18.296 --> 00:08:22.742
Minus 10 cosine becomes plus
10 cosine with 180 degrees

121
00:08:22.782 --> 00:08:28.445
added to the phase, so 10 at 20
Hz with phase plus 120 degrees.

122
00:08:28.495 --> 00:08:30.884
And a sine is a cosine delayed by 90

123
00:08:30.924 --> 00:08:35.161
degrees, so 4 at 60 Hz with
phase minus 90 degrees.

124
00:08:35.211 --> 00:08:40.901
The spectrum is therefore three lines,
at 0, 20 and 60 Hz, each with an

125
00:08:40.941 --> 00:08:46.544
amplitude and a phase; notice also
that the mean of w is 7, the DC term.

126
00:08:48.194 --> 00:08:52.763
Any periodic signal can be decomposed
into an infinite sum of sinusoids at

127
00:08:52.803 --> 00:08:58.261
multiples of the fundamental frequency,
the harmonics: this is the Fourier series.

128
00:08:58.311 --> 00:09:02.815
In compact exponential form, x of
t is the sum over n from minus

129
00:09:02.855 --> 00:09:07.287
to plus infinity of c n times e
to the j two pi n f zero t, and

130
00:09:07.327 --> 00:09:11.689
each coefficient c n is the
integral over one period of x of t

131
00:09:11.729 --> 00:09:16.622
times e to the minus j two pi n
f zero t, divided by the period.

132
00:09:16.672 --> 00:09:20.583
Watch the square wave being
rebuilt: the DC term plus the first

133
00:09:20.623 --> 00:09:25.788
harmonic gives a rough sinusoid, and each
new odd harmonic sharpens the edges.

134
00:09:25.838 --> 00:09:30.256
The term for n equal to zero is the DC
term, and it is just the mean value of

135
00:09:30.296 --> 00:09:34.486
the signal: put n equal to zero in the
coefficient formula and you get the

136
00:09:34.526 --> 00:09:40.421
definition of the mean, which is exactly
what the 2023 partial asked you to prove.

137
00:09:40.471 --> 00:09:43.202
Parseval's theorem gives
the mean power as the

138
00:09:43.242 --> 00:09:46.603
sum of the squared magnitudes
of the coefficients.

139
00:09:46.653 --> 00:09:50.386
Because the sum runs over
negative frequencies too, this is

140
00:09:50.426 --> 00:09:53.904
a two-sided spectrum: a cosine
of amplitude A shows two

141
00:09:53.944 --> 00:09:57.677
lines of A over 2, at plus and
minus f zero, which is why

142
00:09:57.717 --> 00:10:02.352
the official answers draw weights
of one half, as the FAQ explains.

143
00:10:04.002 --> 00:10:08.135
The most important periodic signal
in digital communication is

144
00:10:08.175 --> 00:10:12.699
the train of rectangular pulses,
of duration tau and period T.

145
00:10:12.749 --> 00:10:17.743
Its spectrum has lines every one over
T, whose heights follow the envelope A

146
00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:22.910
tau over T times sinc of f tau, with A
the pulse amplitude, where sinc of x is

147
00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:28.598
sine of pi x divided by pi x; the first
zero of the envelope is at one over tau.

148
00:10:28.648 --> 00:10:32.246
The general lesson, shown in the
notes, is that the more abrupt

149
00:10:32.286 --> 00:10:35.826
the transitions in time, the
wider the spectrum, which matters

150
00:10:35.866 --> 00:10:39.912
a lot when we send digital pulses
through band-limited channels.

151
00:10:39.962 --> 00:10:44.307
Now watch what happens as the period
grows: the lines get closer and

152
00:10:44.347 --> 00:10:49.015
closer, and in the limit, when T goes
to infinity, the signal is a single

153
00:10:49.055 --> 00:10:54.499
pulse and the spectrum becomes continuous;
that limit is the Fourier transform.

154
00:10:54.549 --> 00:11:00.184
And problem 3.3 asks for the mean of this
train: integrate A over one pulse and

155
00:11:00.224 --> 00:11:05.638
divide by the period, which gives A tau
over T, the DC line you see in the middle.

156
00:11:07.288 --> 00:11:09.788
This derivation is short
and appears in the

157
00:11:09.828 --> 00:11:13.130
problem collection, so let
us do it once properly.

158
00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:17.313
By definition, the transform is
the integral of x of t times e to

159
00:11:17.353 --> 00:11:21.615
the minus j two pi f t; the pulse
is 1 between minus tau over 2

160
00:11:21.655 --> 00:11:26.371
and tau over 2, and zero elsewhere,
so only that interval remains.

161
00:11:26.421 --> 00:11:30.532
The integral of the exponential
is immediate, as Barlabé

162
00:11:30.572 --> 00:11:34.401
insists in the FAQ: you get
e to the minus j two pi f t

163
00:11:34.441 --> 00:11:38.482
divided by minus j two pi f,
evaluated between the limits,

164
00:11:38.522 --> 00:11:44.360
which is e to the j pi f tau minus e to
the minus j pi f tau, over j two pi f.

165
00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:48.371
Euler's formula says that the
difference of the two exponentials is

166
00:11:48.411 --> 00:11:53.061
two j times the sine, so the result
is sine of pi f tau divided by pi f.

167
00:11:53.111 --> 00:11:57.806
Multiplying and dividing by tau, we
recognise tau times sinc of f tau,

168
00:11:57.846 --> 00:12:03.202
the official answer, whose first zeros
are at plus and minus one over tau.

169
00:12:03.252 --> 00:12:08.273
And problem 3.4 is free once you have
this: its v of t is a staircase,

170
00:12:08.313 --> 00:12:12.968
height 2 inside tau 1 and height 1
out to tau 2, which is exactly the

171
00:12:13.008 --> 00:12:16.993
sum of two centred unit pulses,
and the transform is linear, so it

172
00:12:17.033 --> 00:12:22.688
becomes the sum of two sincs, tau 1 sinc
of f tau 1 plus tau 2 sinc of f tau 2.

173
00:12:24.338 --> 00:12:28.613
Two more items from the Fourier section
of the notes complete the picture.

174
00:12:28.663 --> 00:12:32.190
Rayleigh's theorem says that
the energy of a signal can be

175
00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:36.004
computed in either domain: the
integral of x squared over time

176
00:12:36.044 --> 00:12:40.576
equals the integral of the squared
modulus of X of f over frequency.

177
00:12:40.626 --> 00:12:44.372
So the squared modulus of the
transform is the energy spectral

178
00:12:44.412 --> 00:12:48.279
density, telling you how the energy
is distributed in frequency;

179
00:12:48.319 --> 00:12:52.760
Parseval's theorem is the same idea
for periodic signals and power.

180
00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:56.663
A computer cannot integrate a
continuous signal, so it uses

181
00:12:56.703 --> 00:13:01.276
the discrete Fourier transform,
the DFT, defined on N samples.

182
00:13:01.326 --> 00:13:05.006
The fast Fourier transform,
the FFT, is not a different

183
00:13:05.046 --> 00:13:08.792
transform but an efficient
algorithm to compute the DFT,

184
00:13:08.832 --> 00:13:12.512
needing about N log N operations
instead of N squared: if

185
00:13:12.552 --> 00:13:17.732
asked, the DFT is the definition and
the FFT is the way to compute it.

186
00:13:19.382 --> 00:13:20.882
Now systems.

187
00:13:20.932 --> 00:13:23.807
A system is linear if it
obeys superposition: the

188
00:13:23.847 --> 00:13:26.485
response to a sum of
inputs is the sum of the

189
00:13:26.525 --> 00:13:30.802
responses, and its input-output
characteristic is a straight line.

190
00:13:30.852 --> 00:13:35.845
The fingerprint of linearity is that
a sinusoid at f zero comes out as a

191
00:13:35.885 --> 00:13:41.198
sinusoid at the same f zero, only
with a different amplitude and phase.

192
00:13:41.248 --> 00:13:44.859
A non-linear system has a curved
characteristic, its output

193
00:13:44.899 --> 00:13:48.138
shape depends on the input
level, and new frequencies

194
00:13:48.178 --> 00:13:52.488
appear at its output, as in an
amplifier driven into saturation.

195
00:13:52.538 --> 00:13:55.873
A linear and time-invariant
system is characterised by

196
00:13:55.913 --> 00:13:59.436
its transfer function H of f,
the transform of the output

197
00:13:59.476 --> 00:14:02.686
divided by the transform of
the input, so the output

198
00:14:02.726 --> 00:14:06.061
spectrum is simply the input
spectrum multiplied by H;

199
00:14:06.101 --> 00:14:09.561
and the range of frequencies
where the response is flat,

200
00:14:09.601 --> 00:14:13.249
usually down to the minus 3
decibel, half-power points,

201
00:14:13.289 --> 00:14:17.826
is the bandwidth, giving low-pass,
band-pass and high-pass systems.

202
00:14:17.876 --> 00:14:21.836
In the time domain the same system
is described by its impulse

203
00:14:21.876 --> 00:14:25.836
response h of t, the inverse
transform of H, and the output is

204
00:14:25.876 --> 00:14:29.771
the convolution of the input
with h, an integral that is much

205
00:14:29.811 --> 00:14:34.805
harder than a multiplication, which
is exactly why we work in frequency.

206
00:14:36.455 --> 00:14:39.038
The handwritten class
notes introduce the Dirac

207
00:14:39.078 --> 00:14:43.168
delta, and the 2023 partial
asked about it directly.

208
00:14:43.218 --> 00:14:47.375
The delta is the limit of a pulse of
width tau and height one over tau

209
00:14:47.415 --> 00:14:51.692
when tau goes to zero, and its defining
property is sifting: integrating

210
00:14:51.732 --> 00:14:56.699
a function times a delta at t zero picks
the value of the function at t zero.

211
00:14:56.749 --> 00:15:02.184
If the input is a delta, the convolution
integral collapses to h of t.

212
00:15:02.234 --> 00:15:06.007
So the output is the impulse
response h A of t, the inverse

213
00:15:06.047 --> 00:15:09.563
Fourier transform of the
transfer function: that is the

214
00:15:09.603 --> 00:15:14.542
answer to question 7, and the reason
for the name impulse response.

215
00:15:14.592 --> 00:15:18.658
Two more identities from the notes:
convolving with a delta returns

216
00:15:18.698 --> 00:15:23.522
the signal itself, and convolving with
a delayed delta delays the signal.

217
00:15:23.572 --> 00:15:27.162
Finally, with deltas we can
transform periodic signals:

218
00:15:27.202 --> 00:15:30.659
the transform of a cosine
is two deltas of weight one

219
00:15:30.699 --> 00:15:35.009
half at plus and minus f zero,
the two-sided spectrum again.

220
00:15:36.659 --> 00:15:39.863
This question appears in
the collection, in 2016

221
00:15:39.903 --> 00:15:43.720
and in 2022, so it is worth
one minute of method.

222
00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:48.577
The first input is a tone at W over
2: its spectrum is two lines at

223
00:15:48.617 --> 00:15:53.745
plus and minus W over 2, inside
the pass band, where H equals one.

224
00:15:53.795 --> 00:15:58.161
The second input is a tone at
twice W: its lines are at plus

225
00:15:58.201 --> 00:16:02.957
and minus two W, outside the
pass band, where H equals zero.

226
00:16:03.007 --> 00:16:07.449
Multiplying spectra, the first
output spectrum equals the input

227
00:16:07.489 --> 00:16:12.749
spectrum, so y 1 of t equals x 1
of t: the tone passes unchanged.

228
00:16:12.799 --> 00:16:18.165
The second output spectrum is zero, so
y 2 of t is zero: the tone is blocked.

229
00:16:18.215 --> 00:16:22.803
And in the 2022 version the input
is the sum of both tones; the

230
00:16:22.843 --> 00:16:27.760
filter is linear, so by superposition
only the tone at B over 2 survives.

231
00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:31.698
This is the mirror image
of the rectangular

232
00:16:31.738 --> 00:16:35.716
pulse, and it was worth one
and a half points in 2022.

233
00:16:35.766 --> 00:16:39.610
The impulse response is the
inverse transform of H, which is

234
00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:44.208
the integral of e to the plus j
two pi f t between minus B and B.

235
00:16:44.258 --> 00:16:49.426
The same algebra as before gives
sine of two pi B t divided by pi t.

236
00:16:49.476 --> 00:16:52.999
Written with the sinc function,
h of t equals 2 B sinc

237
00:16:53.039 --> 00:16:56.561
of 2 B t: a central peak of
height 2 B at t equal to

238
00:16:56.601 --> 00:17:00.832
zero, and zeros at every non-zero
multiple of one over 2 B.

239
00:17:00.882 --> 00:17:04.443
Remember the duality: a rectangle
in time gives a sinc in

240
00:17:04.483 --> 00:17:07.917
frequency, and a rectangle
in frequency gives a sinc in

241
00:17:07.957 --> 00:17:11.455
time; notice also that this
ideal filter responds before

242
00:17:11.495 --> 00:17:15.698
the impulse arrives, which is
why it cannot be built exactly.

243
00:17:17.348 --> 00:17:20.709
Again, spectra multiply, so
the output occupies only the

244
00:17:20.749 --> 00:17:24.803
frequencies that are both in the
signal and in the pass band.

245
00:17:24.853 --> 00:17:30.521
The signal occupies from zero up to W,
and the filter passes from B 1 to B 2.

246
00:17:30.571 --> 00:17:35.206
Since B 1 is below W and W is
below B 2, the overlap goes

247
00:17:35.246 --> 00:17:40.731
from B 1 to W, shown in orange,
mirrored at negative frequencies.

248
00:17:40.781 --> 00:17:45.560
So the output is a band-pass signal
with bandwidth W minus B 1; draw it

249
00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:51.429
exactly like this on the exam, with both
the positive and negative frequency bands.

250
00:17:53.079 --> 00:17:56.425
Problem 3.7 is the reverse
situation, and the

251
00:17:56.465 --> 00:18:00.430
collection asks you to draw the
answer, not just give a number.

252
00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:05.749
Spectra multiply: the output spectrum
is X of f times H of f, so X survives

253
00:18:05.789 --> 00:18:11.732
only where the filter equals one, that
is between minus f zero and plus f zero.

254
00:18:11.782 --> 00:18:16.079
The positive band runs from f
zero minus B to f zero plus B,

255
00:18:16.119 --> 00:18:20.199
so the filter cuts it exactly
at its centre, and only the

256
00:18:20.239 --> 00:18:25.576
inner half, from f zero minus B to
f zero, survives, shown in orange.

257
00:18:25.626 --> 00:18:29.192
By symmetry, at negative
frequencies only the piece

258
00:18:29.232 --> 00:18:33.116
from minus f zero to minus
f zero plus B survives.

259
00:18:33.166 --> 00:18:38.057
So draw each piece with the original
sloping inner edge and flat top, ending in

260
00:18:38.097 --> 00:18:43.315
a vertical wall at plus and minus f zero,
exactly as in the official solution.

261
00:18:44.965 --> 00:18:49.093
Modulation is the systematic
alteration of a parameter of a carrier

262
00:18:49.133 --> 00:18:53.651
signal according to the information
carried by a modulating signal.

263
00:18:53.701 --> 00:18:57.613
In telecommunications the carrier
is a sinusoid, because it

264
00:18:57.653 --> 00:19:01.766
occupies the least bandwidth,
and it has only three parameters

265
00:19:01.806 --> 00:19:05.517
to vary: the amplitude, giving
amplitude modulation, the

266
00:19:05.557 --> 00:19:11.199
frequency, giving frequency modulation,
and the phase, giving phase modulation.

267
00:19:11.249 --> 00:19:15.800
The simplest example, double-sideband
amplitude modulation, shows the

268
00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:20.458
essential effect, a translation of
the spectrum: multiplying x of t by

269
00:19:20.498 --> 00:19:24.983
a cosine at f zero gives one half
of X of f minus f zero plus X of f

270
00:19:25.023 --> 00:19:29.441
plus f zero; watch the baseband
spectrum split and move to plus and

271
00:19:29.481 --> 00:19:34.099
minus f zero, now occupying 2 B;
frequency and phase modulation also

272
00:19:34.139 --> 00:19:40.078
move the signal to the carrier, but their
spectra and bandwidths follow other rules.

273
00:19:40.128 --> 00:19:43.871
There are five reasons to modulate:
first, to radiate efficiently: an

274
00:19:43.911 --> 00:19:47.380
antenna must be comparable to the
wavelength, and a voice signal

275
00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:54.891
between 300 and 3 400 Hz has wavelengths
from 1 000 km down to 88 km,

276
00:19:54.931 --> 00:20:01.585
while at 130 MHz, in the aeronautical
band, the wavelength is only 2.3 m.

277
00:20:01.635 --> 00:20:05.976
Second, to avoid hardware limitations;
third, to reduce noise and

278
00:20:06.016 --> 00:20:10.424
interference, as frequency modulation
does; fourth, to assign each

279
00:20:10.464 --> 00:20:15.801
station its own band; and fifth, to
multiplex many signals on one channel.

280
00:20:17.451 --> 00:20:19.461
Distortion is any difference between the

281
00:20:19.501 --> 00:20:22.259
shape of the output and
the shape of the input.

282
00:20:22.309 --> 00:20:26.153
A system does not distort if the
output is the input scaled by a

283
00:20:26.193 --> 00:20:29.794
constant K and delayed by t
zero, which in frequency means a

284
00:20:29.834 --> 00:20:34.978
transfer function of constant modulus and
a phase that is a straight line in f.

285
00:20:35.028 --> 00:20:37.779
If the modulus is not
constant we have amplitude

286
00:20:37.819 --> 00:20:42.123
distortion, and if the phase is not
linear we have phase distortion.

287
00:20:42.173 --> 00:20:46.167
Phase distortion is measured with the
phase delay and the group delay; in a

288
00:20:46.207 --> 00:20:50.592
distortionless system they are equal,
and equal to the delay of the signal.

289
00:20:50.642 --> 00:20:54.330
Linear distortion appears in linear
systems and can be corrected:

290
00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:58.116
the handwritten notes show an
equalizer whose transfer function is

291
00:20:58.156 --> 00:21:02.521
a constant divided by that of the
amplifier, so their product is flat.

292
00:21:02.571 --> 00:21:07.138
The bandwidth is the range where
amplitude and phase distortion stay below

293
00:21:07.178 --> 00:21:13.426
some limit, and it must fit the signal:
about 5 MHz for video, 3.1 kHz for

294
00:21:13.466 --> 00:21:18.548
voice, and at least r over 2 for a
digital signal of r symbols per second.

295
00:21:20.198 --> 00:21:23.739
Non-linear distortion is
produced by non-linear systems;

296
00:21:23.779 --> 00:21:27.966
it can be severe and it cannot
be corrected with equalizers.

297
00:21:28.016 --> 00:21:30.838
If a single tone enters,
the output contains the

298
00:21:30.878 --> 00:21:33.640
tone plus harmonics at
integer multiples of its

299
00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:37.666
frequency, and the total harmonic
distortion summarises them.

300
00:21:37.716 --> 00:21:42.272
If two close tones enter, besides
their harmonics we get intermodulation

301
00:21:42.312 --> 00:21:46.931
products at combinations plus or
minus k f 1 plus or minus l f 2; the

302
00:21:46.971 --> 00:21:52.683
third-order ones, like 2 f 1 minus f 2,
fall right next to the wanted signals.

303
00:21:52.733 --> 00:21:57.791
So in the 2023 partial, a 1 kHz
tone entering an amplifier and

304
00:21:57.831 --> 00:22:01.810
coming out with a 2 kHz tone
is non-linear distortion, more

305
00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:05.348
precisely harmonic distortion,
because a frequency appeared

306
00:22:05.388 --> 00:22:10.057
that was not at the input; a linear
system can never create frequencies.

307
00:22:10.107 --> 00:22:15.489
Problem 3.6 is the same idea with
algebra: squaring a cosine gives one half

308
00:22:15.529 --> 00:22:20.039
plus one half cosine of twice the
frequency, so the output spectrum has a

309
00:22:20.079 --> 00:22:25.352
line of one half at zero and lines of
one quarter at plus and minus 2 f zero.

310
00:22:25.402 --> 00:22:27.674
And a non-linear system has no transfer

311
00:22:27.714 --> 00:22:30.849
function at all, which
is the answer to part c.

312
00:22:32.499 --> 00:22:36.773
Noise is an unwanted random
signal that is always present.

313
00:22:36.823 --> 00:22:40.021
Any resistor above absolute
zero shows a random voltage

314
00:22:40.061 --> 00:22:43.436
between its terminals, due to
the thermal agitation of its

315
00:22:43.476 --> 00:22:46.851
electrons: this is thermal
noise, the most important kind,

316
00:22:46.891 --> 00:22:51.245
and other noise sources are usually
treated as if they were thermal.

317
00:22:51.295 --> 00:22:55.002
Its spectrum is flat over the
communication bands, hence the

318
00:22:55.042 --> 00:22:58.875
name white noise, and its power
is k T B: Boltzmann's constant

319
00:22:58.915 --> 00:23:03.638
times the absolute temperature in
kelvin times the bandwidth in hertz.

320
00:23:03.688 --> 00:23:09.328
Careful with the constant: the notes
and the 2023 exam print 1.37 times

321
00:23:09.368 --> 00:23:14.787
ten to the minus 23 joules per kelvin,
while the accepted value is 1.38;

322
00:23:14.827 --> 00:23:19.258
use whatever the exam gives you, the
difference is negligible in decibels.

323
00:23:19.308 --> 00:23:23.402
Since noise cannot be removed, we
live with it, and what matters

324
00:23:23.442 --> 00:23:27.341
is the signal to noise ratio S
over N: with a large ratio the

325
00:23:27.381 --> 00:23:30.893
noise is imperceptible, with
a small ratio it masks the

326
00:23:30.933 --> 00:23:36.191
information, and in digital systems it
raises the probability of bit errors.

327
00:23:37.841 --> 00:23:40.057
Since you cannot bring
a formula sheet, here

328
00:23:40.097 --> 00:23:42.754
is the decibel toolkit
you need in your head.

329
00:23:42.804 --> 00:23:45.748
A ratio in decibels is
10 times the base-ten

330
00:23:45.788 --> 00:23:49.461
logarithm of the power ratio,
so doubling is about 3

331
00:23:49.501 --> 00:23:54.891
dB, four times is about 6 dB,
and ten times is exactly 10 dB.

332
00:23:54.941 --> 00:23:59.311
An absolute power in dBm is
referred to one milliwatt: 0 dBm

333
00:23:59.351 --> 00:24:03.851
is one milliwatt, 30 dBm is
one watt, minus 30 dBm is one

334
00:24:03.891 --> 00:24:08.980
microwatt, minus 60 is one nanowatt
and minus 90 is one picowatt.

335
00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:13.839
In decibels, products become sums,
so a received power in dBm is the

336
00:24:13.879 --> 00:24:18.550
transmitted power in dBm, plus both
directivities in dB, minus the

337
00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:23.261
free-space loss, plus the loss
coefficients in dB, which are zero or

338
00:24:23.301 --> 00:24:28.595
negative: a wall that attenuates
5 dB is a coefficient of minus 5

339
00:24:28.635 --> 00:24:33.306
dB, which you add, so never subtract
a negative number; and a signal

340
00:24:33.346 --> 00:24:38.892
to noise ratio in decibels is the
signal in dBm minus the noise in dBm.

341
00:24:38.942 --> 00:24:42.599
One more rule, from the antenna
notes: in free space, doubling

342
00:24:42.639 --> 00:24:46.952
the distance divides the power by
four, minus 6 dB, and since the

343
00:24:46.992 --> 00:24:51.127
noise does not change with
distance, an S over N of 20 dB at

344
00:24:51.167 --> 00:24:57.241
distance R becomes 14 dB at twice the
distance, as in the 2022 partial.

345
00:24:57.291 --> 00:25:00.940
But the Friis equation
itself, as Barlabé stresses

346
00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:04.082
in the FAQ, must be
evaluated in linear units,

347
00:25:04.122 --> 00:25:08.229
watts and metres, so convert
every decibel value first.

348
00:25:09.879 --> 00:25:12.969
Two quick noise questions
from recent partials.

349
00:25:13.019 --> 00:25:16.801
In 2022, noise power is
proportional to the absolute

350
00:25:16.841 --> 00:25:20.328
temperature, so convert to
kelvin: 17 degrees Celsius

351
00:25:20.368 --> 00:25:26.148
is 290 kelvin and 34 degrees
Celsius is 307 kelvin;

352
00:25:26.188 --> 00:25:30.007
doubling the Celsius temperature
does not double the noise.

353
00:25:30.057 --> 00:25:36.565
The ratio 307 over 290 is
only plus 0.25 dB, so the

354
00:25:36.605 --> 00:25:43.445
noise goes from minus 114
to about minus 113.75 dBm.

355
00:25:43.495 --> 00:25:49.361
In 2023, compute k T B: 1.37
times ten to the minus 23, times

356
00:25:49.401 --> 00:25:56.116
290, times 4 million, which is 1.59
times ten to the minus 14 watts.

357
00:25:56.166 --> 00:26:02.786
In dBm, divide by one milliwatt
and take 10 log: minus 108 dBm.

358
00:26:02.836 --> 00:26:05.749
The signal is 0 dBm,
so the signal to noise

359
00:26:05.789 --> 00:26:11.614
ratio is 0 minus minus
108, which is 108 dB.

360
00:26:13.264 --> 00:26:16.227
Here is the one fundamental
advantage of digital

361
00:26:16.267 --> 00:26:19.745
over analog, and it is a
guaranteed exam question.

362
00:26:19.795 --> 00:26:23.628
Along a channel the signal is
attenuated but the noise is not,

363
00:26:23.668 --> 00:26:28.016
so repeaters are installed every
so often to amplify the signal.

364
00:26:28.066 --> 00:26:31.782
The handwritten notes show the
problem: an ideal amplifier

365
00:26:31.822 --> 00:26:35.733
multiplies signal and noise by
the same gain and keeps S over

366
00:26:35.773 --> 00:26:39.748
N, but a real amplifier adds its
own noise, so after every hop

367
00:26:39.788 --> 00:26:44.542
the signal to noise ratio gets worse,
and there is no way to fix it.

368
00:26:44.592 --> 00:26:48.597
Watch the analog chain on the top
row: after each hop the waveform is

369
00:26:48.637 --> 00:26:53.267
noisier, which limits the maximum
distance for a given transmitter power.

370
00:26:53.317 --> 00:26:57.624
A digital signal, however, can only
take a few known levels, so if the

371
00:26:57.664 --> 00:27:01.909
noise is not excessive each repeater
decides which level was sent and

372
00:27:01.949 --> 00:27:06.832
rebuilds a clean signal: that is a
regenerative repeater, the bottom row.

373
00:27:06.882 --> 00:27:09.961
So in a digital system there
is virtually no limit to the

374
00:27:10.001 --> 00:27:12.860
distance, and that is the
answer to 'what is the main

375
00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:17.298
advantage of digital communication'
and 'what is a regenerative repeater'.

376
00:27:18.948 --> 00:27:23.455
The third limitation, after distortion
and noise, is interference.

377
00:27:23.505 --> 00:27:27.638
Interference is the contamination
of an information signal by another

378
00:27:27.678 --> 00:27:32.504
similar signal, normally of human
origin, for example another transmitter.

379
00:27:32.554 --> 00:27:37.676
It is frequent and often very hard to
remove in radio, and less common on lines.

380
00:27:37.726 --> 00:27:41.792
On lines, the typical form is
crosstalk, diafonia: the conversation

381
00:27:41.832 --> 00:27:46.226
on one pair of a cable is heard in
the background of another pair.

382
00:27:46.276 --> 00:27:49.646
It is caused by the partial
capacitances between the conductors

383
00:27:49.686 --> 00:27:53.662
in the cable and is avoided by a
good choice of transmission lines.

384
00:27:55.312 --> 00:27:59.608
The two main limitations of a
telecommunication system are bandwidth and

385
00:27:59.648 --> 00:28:04.907
noise, and Shannon combined them in
1948 in the Hartley-Shannon law: the

386
00:28:04.947 --> 00:28:09.512
capacity C equals B times the base-two
logarithm of one plus S over N.

387
00:28:09.562 --> 00:28:13.066
The capacity is the maximum
amount of information per second

388
00:28:13.106 --> 00:28:16.432
that the channel can carry:
above it, errors are certain;

389
00:28:16.472 --> 00:28:20.779
below it, error-free communication
is possible, at least in theory.

390
00:28:20.829 --> 00:28:24.643
Capacity is directly proportional
to the bandwidth but grows only

391
00:28:24.683 --> 00:28:29.004
logarithmically with the signal to
noise ratio, as the curve shows.

392
00:28:29.054 --> 00:28:33.432
For example, a 25 kHz aeronautical
channel with an S over N of

393
00:28:33.472 --> 00:28:38.940
30 dB, which is a ratio of 1 000,
gives 25 000 times log base 2

394
00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:47.133
of 1 001, which is 249.2 kbit/s,
the answer to problem 3.11 c.

395
00:28:47.183 --> 00:28:51.808
And the new 8.33 kHz
channels of the 2023 partial

396
00:28:51.848 --> 00:28:56.496
give 83.1 kbit/s at the
same signal to noise ratio.

397
00:28:58.146 --> 00:29:01.468
Three short capacity questions
that keep coming back.

398
00:29:01.518 --> 00:29:06.245
Problem 3.9: white noise power is
proportional to the bandwidth,

399
00:29:06.285 --> 00:29:10.083
so with the same signal power,
dividing the bandwidth by three

400
00:29:10.123 --> 00:29:14.107
divides the noise by three, and
the signal to noise ratio becomes

401
00:29:14.147 --> 00:29:19.202
3 A; Barlabé's FAQ says it bluntly,
it is much simpler than it looks.

402
00:29:19.252 --> 00:29:25.033
Problem 3.10, and question 7 of
2016: if you double the bandwidth

403
00:29:25.073 --> 00:29:29.261
you also double the noise, so the
capacity becomes 2 B times log of

404
00:29:29.301 --> 00:29:33.940
one plus S over 2 N, which is less
than twice the original capacity.

405
00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:38.170
With an S over N of 100, doubling
the bandwidth multiplies

406
00:29:38.210 --> 00:29:44.115
the capacity by 1.70 and tripling
it by 2.30, so the capacity

407
00:29:44.155 --> 00:29:48.597
increases but is not proportional;
justify it exactly like this.

408
00:29:48.647 --> 00:29:53.126
Problem 3.12: in a radio link
the received signal falls

409
00:29:53.166 --> 00:29:56.741
as one over R squared while
the noise stays constant, so

410
00:29:56.781 --> 00:30:01.327
the capacity is B log 2 of one
plus K 1 over K 2 R squared.

411
00:30:01.377 --> 00:30:05.097
When S over N is much larger than
one, this becomes a constant

412
00:30:05.137 --> 00:30:09.099
plus a constant times log 2 of
one over R: every doubling of the

413
00:30:09.139 --> 00:30:13.673
distance costs 2 B bits per second,
which is the answer in the key.

414
00:30:15.323 --> 00:30:17.519
Let us summarise part two.

415
00:30:17.569 --> 00:30:20.979
You know the I T U definition,
the general model with the

416
00:30:21.019 --> 00:30:24.490
channel's attenuation and
bandwidth, the duplex modes with

417
00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:28.995
the pilot and controller as
half-duplex, and the NDB as broadcast.

418
00:30:29.045 --> 00:30:33.757
You can read and draw spectra, prove
that the DC coefficient is the mean,

419
00:30:33.797 --> 00:30:38.124
transform a rectangle into a sinc
and back, push tones through ideal

420
00:30:38.164 --> 00:30:43.908
filters, and say that a delta at the input
gives the impulse response at the output.

421
00:30:43.958 --> 00:30:47.382
You know that modulation
translates the spectrum, that

422
00:30:47.422 --> 00:30:50.526
linear distortion is
correctable while non-linear

423
00:30:50.566 --> 00:30:54.311
distortion creates new frequencies,
that thermal noise is k

424
00:30:54.351 --> 00:30:59.320
T B, and why regenerative repeaters
make digital communication superior.

